# Goose (aaif-goose/goose) as a harness in a Shards microVM: the Agentfile kiln ships for it (plan
# 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 23; research 01a1ce705e3d72af8563e07fa92a7460, f1-f28).
# Its build context is what `kiln agentfiles context goose DIR` writes: this file, and for each
# platform goose-<arch>/ with harness.json and pin.json's goose, checked and unpacked to bin/goose.
# It holds no credential: a person's provider keys stay on their own volume (below).

# The default base: Debian with git, an ssh client, curl and CA certificates on both arches, the
# digest pin.sh read (pin.json's base). Any base works, as Goose's build is static (f10), but Goose
# checks TLS against the base's CA bundle, so a base without one can't reach a provider (f18).
ARG BASE=buildpack-deps:trixie-scm@sha256:4f34e1d673fdb65371de9f88bdd32c4e30152fe692b35604aef5c0b3c0e6da10
FROM ${BASE}

# Goose's own static build for the platform being built: no RUN, and no libc variant (f10, f23).
ARG TARGETARCH
HARNESS goose FROM ./goose-${TARGETARCH}

# Its network, every part named (default deny; Shards D236, destinations by name): a network for this
# harness alone, which it joins with no flow to any member, and TCP out to the hosts below alone, by
# name, through the network's boundary (--egress) and the microVM's (EXPOSE ... AS egress). Each name
# resolves for it through the agents' resolver, and no other does. Nothing comes in; no other host,
# port or protocol goes out. 443 is HTTPS's port (RFC 9110 4.2.2). Each host is cited to its vendor's own
# documentation, or, where that names a wildcard or nothing, to the vendor's own source or build, in its
# harness's record's "Hosts by name" (kiln plan 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 18, part 4):
# docs/research/s23-goose-agentfile/README.md and its evidence/hosts/hosts.tsv: every provider Goose 1.59.0
# reaches at a fixed public host, with the sign-in hosts of those it signs in to itself, and TypeSafe's for its auto
# effort. Not granted: a provider whose account, resource or region places its host (Bedrock, SageMaker, Azure,
# Databricks, Snowflake, Vertex AI, Tanzu); a local or self-hosted server (Ollama, LM Studio, LiteLLM ...); and a host
# the person gives (an environment variable's, a custom provider's). Each of those needs an Agentfile of your own,
# made from this one.
# Each host is cited to its provider's own API docs and to the harness's own source, or to that source alone where
# no page of the provider's own docs names it (ROOT's call of 2026-11-13, the person's bar: zero breaks against
# today); kiln's launch is to keep only the session's own provider's hosts (plan step 17). Each host, by its provider:
#   ai-gateway.vercel.sh (Vercel AI Gateway), ai.salad.cloud (SaladCloud AI Gateway), aihubmix.com (AIHubMix),
#   api.aigateway.sh (AIgateway), api.aimlapi.com (AI/ML API), api.anthropic.com (Anthropic), api.avian.io (Avian),
#   api.cerebras.ai (Cerebras), api.deepseek.com (DeepSeek), api.empiriolabs.ai (EmpirioLabs AI), api.eurouter.ai
#   (EUrouter), api.fireworks.ai (Fireworks AI), api.friendli.ai (Friendli AI), api.github.com (GitHub Copilot
#   token), api.githubcopilot.com (GitHub Copilot), api.gondola-ai.com (Gondola), api.groq.com (Groq),
#   api.inceptionlabs.ai (Inception), api.intelligence.io.solutions (IO Intelligence), api.kimi.com (Kimi Code),
